WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.960
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.000
And today we're here to answer one question:
what is a convolution?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.930
I have heard many university students crying
in despair over their perilous journeys of

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:21.720
understanding what convolutions are, and why
they are useful. Let me give a helping hand here!

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.670
A convolution is a mathematical technique
to mix together two signals. A lot of really

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:32.759
useful tasks can be accomplished through this
operation. For instance, convolutions can

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:38.989
be used to add reverberation to a recorded
instrument. So I play my guitar here in my

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:45.109
room, and it can sound like as if it were
recorded in a large concert hall. Now, dear

00:00:45.109 --> 00:00:50.420
Fellow Scholars, put on a pair of headphones,
and let me rock out on my guitar to show it

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:51.170
to you!

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.440
First, you'll hear the dry guitar signal.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.680
And this is the same signal with the added reverberation.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.350
It sounds much more convincing, right?

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:22.140
In simple words, a convolution is a bit like
saying guitar plus concert hall equals a guitar

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.820
sound that was played in a concert hall. The
only difference is that we don't say guitar

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:34.560
"plus" concert hall, we say guitar "convolved"
with the concert hall. If we want to be a

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.810
bit more accurate, we could say the impulse
response of the hall, which records how this

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:44.950
place reacts to a person who starts to play
the guitar in there. People use the living

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.790
hell out of convolution reverberation plugins
in the music industry.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.480
Convolutions can also be used to blur or sharpen
an image.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.710
We also had many examples of convolutional
neural networks that provide efficient means

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.370
to, for instance, get machines to recognize
traffic signs.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:08.390
We can also use them to add sophisticated
light transport effects, such as subsurface

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:14.379
scattering to images. This way, we can conjure
up digital characters with stunningly high

00:02:14.379 --> 00:02:20.249
quality skin and other translucent materials
in our animations and computer games. We have

00:02:20.249 --> 00:02:24.980
had a previous episode on this, and it is
available in the video description box, make

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.329
sure to have a look!

00:02:26.329 --> 00:02:33.029
As we said before, computing a convolution
is not at all like addition. Not even close.

00:02:33.029 --> 00:02:40.450
For instance, the convolution of two boxes
is ... a triangle. Wow, what? What kind of

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:44.379
witchcraft is this? It doesn't sound intuitive
at all!

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:49.879
The computation of the convolution means that
we start to push this box over the other one

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:55.549
and at every point in time, we take a look
at the intersection between the two signals.

00:02:55.549 --> 00:03:01.519
As you can see, at first, they don't touch
at all. Then, as they start to overlap, we

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:06.700
have highlighted the intersected area with
green, and as they get closer to each other,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:12.040
this area increases. When they are completely
overlapped, we get the maximum intersection

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.999
area, which then starts to dwindle as they
separate.

00:03:14.999 --> 00:03:19.459
So there you have it. It is the miracle of
mathematics that by computing things like

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:26.010
this, we can rock out in a virtual chuch or
a stadium which sounds very close to the real deal.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.870
And before we go, a quick shoutout to immersive
math, a really intuitive resource for learning

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:37.779
linear algebra. If you are into math, you
simply have to check this one out, it's really cool.

00:03:37.779 --> 00:03:41.719
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

